WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.140
But I wanted to ask you when did you first become aware that there was pollution in Mossville?

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.540
I would say in the '80s.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.600
I would . . . Around the middle '80s.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.980
What specific event kind of . . .

5
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:28.640
Well, they had some spills, toxic spills,

6
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:40.680
and the people in the area where it spilled across the tracks were concerned about their health and the conditions.

7
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.600
So that's when it really kicked off.

8
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.220
When you say "across the tracks," what does that mean?

9
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.440
That was east of here.

10
00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.260
When I say "across the tracks," that's the east part of . . .

11
00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.720
You mean like Bel Air?

12
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.780
Yeah, Bel Air area and the east part of Mossville.

13
00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.920
Okay.

14
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:14.500
That's when we were contacted by some people who were interested in exposing it.

15
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:21.120
And they started what they called the Bucket Brigade.

16
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:29.460
So we were empowered with the knowledge of how the Bucket Brigade worked.

17
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:29.660
We were empowered with . . . See at that time they had those . . . what did they call them?

18
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.340
We started off with pagers.

19
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:43.250
So people would call a certain number and let us know that they smelled whatever they smelled.

20
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:57.790
Shirley and myself were the principal persons who checked on calls that we got from a pager about conditions somewhere in the community.

21
00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:05.050
So whenever they called, we would go out to that area and take a sample.

22
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:12.930
We had our buckets, and we would take a sample of whatever we smelled.

23
00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:25.270
The sample was mailed to California, to a lab in California, and they would check it and send the results back.

24
00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:30.730
So that would go on any time of night or day that we'd get a call.

25
00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:36.270
We'd have to run out and check and send the samples in to the lab.

26
00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.670
So that's how we started.

27
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:59.710
A lot of times the company that we were checking on would call the cops and the cops sought after us or send some of their security out to try to chase us off to keep us from getting samples of the mess that they were dumping.

28
00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:05.710
So that would go on on a regular basis quite a bit.

29
00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:15.150
We were getting a lot of good samples and a lot of results, and they were getting real shaky about us doing what we were doing.

30
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.150
So that was the beginning of the Bucket Brigade.

31
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.790
I definitely want to ask you more about that.

32
00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.850
But back to like the 1980s.

33
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:30.110
What was spilled [loud cough] that first time?

34
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:40.110
I think it was dioxin and a lot of other chemicals that they were not revealing what they were.

35
00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.470
So it was quite a bit that we don't know about.

36
00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.230
What is dioxin?

37
00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:54.770
It's a deadly chemical that’s dealing with plastics.

38
00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.790
That's the main thing.

39
00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:06.510
It's a deadly plastic chemical that's produced for many purposes around here.

40
00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:11.410
So that's in the air for the people to breathe.

41
00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:18.490
When did the community first find out that there . . . that it was here in the air?

42
00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:25.290
During the Bucket Brigade investigations, which was quite a few.

43
00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.550
That's when it was found out.

44
00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:30.890
What was the reaction?

45
00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:36.370
Well, disbelief from a lot of people.

46
00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:47.330
Lies from the refineries, mainly through employees that worked for them who don't want to lose their jobs.

47
00:04:47.330 --> 00:05:01.090
So that's the basis for their so-called investigations and lies about it not being poisonous and all of this.

48
00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:06.030
They even lied to the employees.

49
00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.770
They don't tell them what they're handling a lot of times.

50
00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.510
They don't need masks and stuff like that.

51
00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:22.310
So a lot of these employees didn't live long after they were exposed.

52
00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.190
A lot of them have passed away.

53
00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.230
If you don't mind me asking, you know what causes . . .

54
00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:27.750
Have what?

55
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.330
Causes of death, do you . . .

56
00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:42.410
Oh, it's a lot of different causes . . . endometriosis . . . it's a lot of names that I can't recall at this time.

57
00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:47.090
But there's plenty that we don't know about, too.

58
00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:58.330
All we know is that people are disappearing because of the ailments that they're getting . . . receiving from these refineries.

59
00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:03.090
A lot of people bring the poison home with them in their clothes,

60
00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:13.330
because they handling all of this poison and they don't change their clothes when they leave the refinery.

61
00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:20.870
They'll wear them home and expose their children and their family to them, and before you know it they're sick.

62
00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:23.470
So a lot of that's happening, too.

63
00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:27.290
Can you tell us a little bit more about the Bucket Brigade, kind of how it started.

64
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:36.930
Well they started . . . I don't remember the names of the people who were involved.

65
00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:41.790
But they started with a group in California.

66
00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:48.390
They designed the buckets and the strategy on getting the information.

67
00:06:48.390 --> 00:07:05.210
So they came here and gave classes to . . . I think it was about three or four of us on how to operate the buckets and what to do with the material once you gathered the intelligence,

68
00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:15.950
And the lab sends the results back to MEAN [Mossville Environmental Action Now], the organization that's representing Mossville.

69
00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:26.130
Then they would determine what it was that we picked up, what time it was, where it was, and the like.

70
00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:35.850
So we would be gathering all that information to prove that one refinery or another were dumping poison on the people.

71
00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:39.070
But we're making quite a bit of progress.

72
00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:47.350
The refineries were getting really upset because of the information we were gathering.

73
00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:53.150
So they started doing more crooked stuff . . . More lies and more deceits.

74
00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:56.570
When they saw y’all out there with the buckets, what would they do?

75
00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:57.850
They would panic.

76
00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:01.870
They would get their little crew out, some of their agents.

77
00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.950
They would call the police, sheriff department.

78
00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:15.510
But by that time we would have our sample and gone, because we had strategies set up to find out just where it was coming from,

79
00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:21.030
how to go in and get the sample as quick as we can and gone.

80
00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:23.170
So that's the way that would be working.

81
00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.570
We would move pretty fast.

82
00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:26.770
So y’all had a plan beforehand?

83
00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:28.350
Yes.

84
00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.370
We had a working plan.

85
00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:35.650
Most times it would be at night, because that's when they would dump their poison,

86
00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:39.530
at night, like a rainy night . . . stuff like that.

87
00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:45.630
So we would be out there in the rain and cold and whatever it took to get those samples.

88
00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:52.530
So a lot of times we would move, it would be mostly me and Shirley.

89
00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:58.810
The way we would do it is she would let me out at a certain spot and she would go down the road.

90
00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:01.990
By the time she'd go down the road, turn around and come back,

91
00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:09.490
I would have the sample and all I would have to do is just jump in the car with her and we'd take off and get it mailed off to them.
